capture: startup-time asset pipeline
runtime: Java 17
commit: migration change set, a verified successor of base commit c8f6226105.
  No hash is recorded here, and none is invented: the revision that carries this
  file cannot be written inside the file it commits.  The relationship was
  measured rather than assumed - git merge-base --is-ancestor reports that the
  base commit is an ancestor of this working tree's head - so the descriptor
  names the revision precisely without fabricating a value for it.  The sibling
  startup/catalina.out.log carries the same descriptor, so both files in this
  directory agree on which revision they describe.
source: application log during container startup, asset-pipeline output relayed at DEBUG level
produced-by: docs/migration/smoke-evidence/smoke-checks.sh
region-status: SKIPPED
skip-reason: TWO preconditions were unmet, and they are named separately because
  they are different failures with different remedies.  Conflating them would
  leave a reader unable to tell why the region is empty, which is the precise
  error this field exists to prevent.
  (1) NO DEPLOYMENT TO OBSERVE.  Measured, not assumed: the documented host name
  arkcase-ce.local does not resolve; there is no servlet-container installation
  on this host, no container launcher on the process path and no launcher script
  anywhere within four directory levels of the filesystem root; CATALINA_LOG,
  TOMCAT_HOME and CATALINA_HOME are all unset; and no container log exists
  anywhere on the filesystem.  The sibling notes/reference-stack.status records
  every reference service answering 000 with no response at all, and
  notes/readiness.status records attempt-1=000.  The producer confirms it from
  its own side: startup/unavailable.log is present in this directory, and that is
  the file it writes precisely when no readable container log is configured.
  ONE DIFFERENCE FROM THE COUNTERPART REGION, RE-MEASURED RATHER THAN COPIED.
  The counterpart additionally recorded that no deployable web archive existed in
  its working tree.  That is NOT true on this side and is deliberately not
  repeated: this checkout does build a deployable web archive, and it is present
  under the application module's build-output directory.  What is missing here is
  a container to deploy it into and the backing services it would talk to, not
  the archive itself.  Copying the counterpart's wording would have understated
  this side's build and overstated its deployment at the same time, which is
  exactly the sort of inherited claim a mirror makes easy to introduce.
  (2) THE COPIER'S OWN LOGGER SITS ABOVE THE RELAY LEVEL.  This is a separate
  failure, and it is the one that would still bite with a container present.
  Every line of relayed pipeline output is written through a DEBUG-level
  output-stream adapter constructed at AngularResourceCopier.java:790, and the
  invocation announcement and completion line of each of the three pairs are
  written at INFO, from the two statements at :779 and :796 respectively.  Both
  levels matter, because the region needs the relayed body and not merely the
  announcements.  That logger's level is not fixed by anything in version control:
  the tracked file
  acm-standard-applications/arkcase/src/main/resources/log4j2-old.xml
  states at :3-4 that the configuration is externalised to
  $HOME/.arkcase/acm/log4j2.xml, and the tracked deployment descriptor
  acm-standard-applications/arkcase/src/main/webapp/META-INF/context.xml mounts
  exactly that external file over the web application's own logging configuration
  through a PreResources file-resource set.  The level is therefore a deployment
  property, supplied from outside the repository.  Host observation, labelled as
  such and NOT a repository fact: the externalised configuration present on this
  host raises com.armedia to debug at its line 35 but then pins the copier's own
  logger to warn with additivity disabled at its line 40, so under that stock
  configuration every announcement, every relayed child line and every completion
  line falls below threshold and is discarded before it reaches any appender.
  THE MIGRATED RUNTIMES ARE DELIBERATELY NOT LISTED among the unmet
  preconditions, because they are not among them.  Java 17 is installed here and
  answers 17.0.19; the Node.js runtime answers v20.20.2 and its package manager
  answers 10.8.2; the sibling env/toolchain.txt records all three banners for
  this side of the capture.  What is missing is the deployment and the logging
  level, not the runtime.  Saying so matters, because a reader could otherwise
  read this skip as the target runtime failing to drive the pipeline, and that is
  the opposite of what was measured.
exit-status-is-not-evidence: true
normalised: nothing, because no bytes were captured.  The policy is stated in
  full anyway, so the mirror reproduces the transformation rather than guessing at
  it, and so a later run has no latitude to invent one.  When this region is
  captured the pipeline STRIPS timestamps and dates, thread names and thread
  identifiers, process identifiers, absolute filesystem paths in favour of a
  stable token, port assignments, elapsed-time and duration figures, per-file byte
  sizes where they vary from run to run, and any progress-percentage churn.  It
  PRESERVES the behaviour-bearing content: task names, the order of the task
  graph, per-task file counts, the identity of each of the three commands, the
  logged exit codes - kept deliberately, as data, so a reader can see for
  themselves that they prove nothing - and the full message text of every warning
  and error line.  Over-normalising this region would destroy the very evidence it
  exists to carry, and that is the dangerous failure rather than the visible one,
  because it makes two captures agree for a reason unrelated to behaviour.
  Two host-specific paths are why the path rule is not optional in this region:
  the staging folder and the deployment folder are configured at
  spring-web-ark-angular-starter.xml:56 and :57 relative to the user's home
  directory, so both appear in the relayed output and neither carries any
  behavioural signal.
  No colour-escape filter is applied, and none is needed.  Re-derived in this
  tree rather than inherited: both task-runner commands carry the no-colour flag,
  at spring-web-ark-angular-starter.xml:46 and :72.  The dependency-install
  command carries no colour flag of its own; its progress suppression moved into
  the child environment instead, where AngularResourceCopier.java:1120 sets the
  package manager's progress configuration to false and :1121 does the same for
  its funding notice.  Confirming an absence is the right response here; adding a
  filter for a condition that does not arise would be an unjustified change.  If
  a future capture of this region does carry an escape sequence, that is itself a
  finding and belongs in this notes block rather than inside a silent filter.
redacted: nothing, because no bytes were captured.  When it is captured, every
  credential-shaped value is replaced with the fixed placeholder <REDACTED>,
  which is the convention this documentation tree already uses; the producer's own
  substitution writes the more specific <REDACTED-CREDENTIAL> form of that same
  token.  The scan covers token assignments, authentication headers and
  configuration keys, password and secret assignments, the user-and-password form
  of a URL authority, and session identifiers.  A dependency install is the
  specific carrier to watch in THIS region, because it can echo a registry token
  or a remote URL with credentials embedded in its authority component.  Two
  in-tree facts narrow that hazard without removing it, and both are recorded
  rather than relied upon: the copier deletes every package-manager run-control
  file it finds in the staging folder before the install runs - the list is at
  AngularResourceCopier.java:103-104 and the removal is announced by the warning
  at :1462 - so a configuration file left lying in that folder cannot contribute a
  token; and the configured registry defaults at :155 to the public registry with
  no credential in it.  The registry is nevertheless a settable property, so the
  scan still covers it.  The documented administrator credential is never
  transcribed here in any form, not even as an illustration of what redaction
  removes.
