notes:
- The capture delimiters above are deliberately empty.  Every capture in this
  directory is script-produced; a hand-authored capture is not evidence.  Because
  the two preconditions recorded in skip-reason were not available, no log line
  was invented to fill the gap.  The fabrication risk in THIS region is sharper
  than anywhere else in the tree, and it is worth naming: forced execution makes a
  broken asset build indistinguishable from a good one by exit code, so the
  captured output is the only discriminator there is.  An invented capture would
  destroy the one signal this gate has, and it would falsely evidence the Node 20
  half of the migration - for which, as the note on module build files below
  records, no other runtime evidence exists anywhere.
- A SKIPPED region is NOT a pass, and two matching SKIPPED regions across the
  baseline and migrated captures are NOT evidence of preserved behaviour.  They
  record an absence in both places, and the only delta between them is the runtime
  label and the one re-measured difference set out in skip-reason.
- Resolving the empty-region ambiguity explicitly, rather than leaving it
  implicit.  An empty region here is ambiguous between "the pipeline never ran"
  and "the pipeline ran and nothing was logged".  On this side BOTH hold, and both
  are named in skip-reason: the pipeline never ran because there is no deployment,
  AND it would have logged nothing at the copier's stock level even if it had.
  Leaving that ambiguity unresolved would be the same class of error the evidence
  rule below exists to prevent - an absence of output silently read as an absence
  of problems.
- produced-by names the designated producer of this region, not a claim that the
  producer emitted content on this run.  It emitted none, which is why the
  delimiters are empty.
- Region-selection rule, stated so this side reproduces the counterpart's region
  boundary rather than guessing at it.  The producer mines the container log with
  a fixed extended-regular-expression alternation, case-insensitively, and writes
  the matched lines under a line cap it announces rather than applies silently.
  The alternation, on one unbroken line so it can be reused verbatim:
  AngularResourceCopier|grunt|node_modules|package-lock
  The cap defaults to 2000 lines and exceeding it marks the run incomplete.  The
  SAME pattern is used on both sides of the mirror, which is what makes the region
  boundary itself identical and a recursive diff mechanically meaningful; a
  pattern varied on one side only would register as a difference carrying no
  behavioural signal at all.
- Field-order rule for the mirror, also stated rather than left to inference.  The
  order is capture, runtime, commit, source, produced-by, region-status, then
  skip-reason when and only when the region is skipped, then
  exit-status-is-not-evidence, normalised and redacted.  skip-reason sits directly
  after the status it explains, matching the sibling startup/catalina.out.log so
  the two files in this directory parse the same way.
  exit-status-is-not-evidence is specific to THIS region and is present on both
  sides regardless of status, because the claim it encodes is a property of the
  pipeline rather than of any particular run.  No field was added that the
  counterpart lacks, and only runtime and commit differ in value by design.
- Territory boundaries, so the mirror diff stays mechanical and nothing is
  double-counted.  This region covers ONLY the three startup-time asset-pipeline
  invocations and the lines immediately around them.  Container and application
  startup belong to startup/catalina.out.log; readiness polling to
  readiness-poll.log; context, aspect and directory-service initialisation to
  spring-context.log, which the producer emits under its own region name
  context-init.log; annotation scanning to reflection-scan.log; persistence
  initialisation to jpa-init.log, which the producer folds into the region it names
  messaging-and-persistence.log; workflow-engine initialisation to
  workflow-engine-init.log, which the producer emits under its region name
  process-definitions.log; the consolidated error-severity scan to error-scan.txt,
  which the producer emits under its region name errors.log; toolchain provenance
  to env/toolchain.txt; archived unit-test reports to surefire/; and the eight
  application flows to the flow captures.  BOTH names are given for every sibling
  whose archive name and producer region name differ - four of them, re-derived
  from the producer's own region declarations rather than assumed - because citing
  only one of a pair would leave a reader unable to match the file to the code that
  writes it.  None of them is duplicated here, and this region claims none of their
  content even where the file itself is not present on this side yet.
- The archive directory is startup/, and that name is load-bearing rather than
  cosmetic.  The repository's root ignore file uses bare, leading-slash-free
  patterns, and git matches those at ANY depth.  Re-verified at this directory's
  exact depth with a .log file placed inside each candidate: a directory named
  logs is ignored by root ignore rule 26, bin by rule 7, target by rule 6, builds
  by rule 40, activemq-data by rule 25 and partition-descriptor by rule 24, while
  this file's own path is reported as not ignored and is therefore committable.
  The ignored token is always the DIRECTORY name and never the .log extension, so
  a .log file placed inside any of those six directories would be silently
  uncommitted while every local check still passed.  This file therefore sits
  directly in startup/ and no subdirectory was created beneath it.  A directory
  named builds is the live temptation for a file about a build, and it is exactly
  one of the six.
- The pointed symmetry is worth recording, because the two ignore files pull in
  opposite directions.  The frontend tree's own ignore file, at
  acm-standard-applications/arkcase/src/main/webapp/resources/.gitignore, contains
  exactly ONE entry - the dependency directory - which is precisely why the new
  lockfile beside it is committable there.  At THIS depth it is the root ignore
  file's bare patterns that force the startup/ directory name.  Reading FROM an
  ignored directory, or from a container's own log directory, is fine; writing
  INTO one is the trap.
- Provenance of the rules invoked throughout these notes, and both facts belong
  together because either one alone misleads.  FIRST, the project's rules facility
  reports, verbatim, "No user rules provided" - there is no on-disk user rules
  document for this project, and that is explicitly NOT licence to lower the bar;
  the work is held to enterprise-standard practice on top of the rules, never
  instead of them.  SECOND, rules are nonetheless present and binding: the
  requirements embed an explicit, numbered block of seven rules that governs this
  work in full exactly as an external rules document would, alongside seven
  transformation rules.  The identifiers the migration plan uses for them are its
  OWN navigational convention rather than quoted titles, which is why every rule
  below is summarised or quoted by its text rather than cited by number alone.  No
  rule was invented and none was softened.
- Governing rule on evidence, verbatim: "Validation asserts on produced artifacts
  and captured output, never on process exit status alone. This is mandatory rather
  than stylistic because Gruntfile.js sets grunt.option('force', true), which masks
  task failures and lets a broken build exit zero."  This region is that rule's
  primary evidence, which is why the header carries exit-status-is-not-evidence as
  a machine-readable field rather than burying the point in prose.
- The chain that rule describes was traced end to end through the code as it
  stands in this migrated tree, and it has five links.  ONE: Gruntfile.js:143-144
  reads, in full, the comment "Making grunt default to force in order not to break
  the project." followed by grunt.option('force', true) - so a failing task does
  not fail the run.  TWO: the task runner therefore exits zero even when
  individual tasks fail.  THREE: the Java side runs each command through the
  process executor's default implementation, which THROWS on a non-zero exit, so a
  zero exit means nothing is thrown; the exit code is taken from that call at
  AngularResourceCopier.java:795 and the stream handler that feeds it is installed
  immediately above at :794.  FOUR: the helper logs done with [{}]: exit code {}
  at :796, with the matching pre-invocation announcement at :779, and startup
  CONTINUES.  FIVE: therefore a broken asset build logs exit code 0, raises
  nothing, and the deployment still reaches ready state.
- Sharpening link THREE, because it is stronger than the rule needs to claim.
  Since a non-zero exit throws before :796 is ever reached, the ONLY exit code
  that line can EVER print is 0.  A logged exit code of 0 in this region is a
  tautology of the control flow, not a finding about the asset build.  The
  single-argument stream handler at :794 receives BOTH the child's standard output
  and its standard error, so a task's error output lands on the same relay as its
  ordinary output and is subject to the same threshold.
- One migrated-side refinement that does NOT close the masking hole, recorded
  because a reader could reasonably assume it does.  This tree adds a failure path
  the base commit did not have: the helper now tees the child's output into a tail
  buffer at :785 and :791, and on failure logs it at ERROR at :802 before
  rethrowing at :805 with the tool's own output carried in the message.  That is a
  real diagnostic improvement for a command that exits NON-zero.  It is precisely
  no help against forced execution, because forced execution is what stops a
  failed task from producing a non-zero exit in the first place - so the ERROR at
  :802 never fires for the failure mode this region exists to guard against.  The
  artifacts remain the only discriminator.
- Two pre-existing defects are masked by exactly that forced-execution setting,
  and they are recorded here as proof that the masking is real rather than
  theoretical.  Both were re-verified by reading Gruntfile.js in this tree.
  FIRST: the alias registered at :379 as sync-dev runs concurrent:default, while
  the concurrent configuration block at :90-98 defines exactly one target, named
  default1 at :91 - so the alias names a target that does not exist.  SECOND: the
  alias registered at :372 as lint runs a JavaScript linter followed by a
  stylesheet linter, but only one of the two is configured.  Re-derived greps
  rather than recollection: searching the file for the JavaScript linter's name
  returns a single occurrence, at :372; searching for the stylesheet linter's name
  returns :40, :42 and :372, its configuration block being :40-47.  The JavaScript
  linter therefore has no configuration block anywhere in the file.
- Both aliases are OUTSIDE the default task graph at Gruntfile.js:376, so NEITHER
  affects the five produced artifacts.  Saying so explicitly keeps the record
  accurate and forestalls a reasonable fear that the build outputs are
  compromised: the value of these two defects is not that they break the build, it
  is that they demonstrate a real, in-tree path by which a broken frontend build
  exits zero.  Under the rule on discovered defects they are REGISTERED, NOT
  FIXED, and nothing in this file proposes, implies or performs a repair to either
  one.
- This region exists at all because the asset pipeline runs INSIDE the deploying
  application at container startup, not at build time.  Re-measured in this tree:
  across all 145 module build files, a search for an asset-pipeline build plugin,
  a runtime-version declaration, or an invocation of the task runner or either
  package manager returns ZERO files.  The pipeline is driven instead by a
  servlet-context-aware Spring bean, declared at
  spring-web-ark-angular-starter.xml:43 and implemented by
  AngularResourceCopier.java, whose class declaration at :92 carries that
  interface.  THE CONSEQUENCE IS THIS GATE'S WHOLE JUSTIFICATION: the deployment
  gate is the ONLY place the rewritten runtime frontend-build wiring is exercised
  at all, so if this region is missing or hollow the Node 20 half of the migration
  has no runtime evidence anywhere.
- An anchor-drift disclosure, made rather than left for a reader to trip over.
  The counterpart region cites its anchors at base commit c8f6226105, which is the
  only revision at which a baseline is meaningful.  This side cites anchors as they
  stand in the migrated tree, and two of the three files involved were rewritten
  by this change set, so their line numbers MOVED.  The three pipeline invocations
  sat at AngularResourceCopier.java:131, :168 and :203 at the base commit and now
  sit at :216, :231 and :266; the three command properties sat at
  spring-web-ark-angular-starter.xml:33-35, :36-38 and :28-30 and now sit at
  :68-70, :71-73 and :45-47; the copy manifest sat at :76-86 and now sits at
  :111-121; the keep-list predicate sat at :153-160 and now sits at :302-325.
  Inside the command helper the same drift applies: the helper was declared at
  :325 with its announcement at :327, its DEBUG adapter at :335, its stream
  handler at :338, its executor call at :339 and its completion line at :340, and
  those now sit at :777, :779, :790, :794, :795 and :796 respectively.
  Gruntfile.js did not move at all, which is itself a finding and is recorded
  under the note on holding versions below.  Both sets are given so neither side
  of the mirror cites a line that does not exist in the tree it describes.
- expected-evidence, and every line so labelled is an EXPECTATION derived by
  reading this repository, never an observation of a run.  Nothing below was
  witnessed on this host.  The wiring anchors, the task graph, the forced-execution
  setting, the two masked aliases and the determinism findings are all true of the
  repository regardless of whether the capture ran, which is why they are recorded
  even though the region is empty - the mirror needs them to select the same region
  and to read it the same way.
- expected-evidence: the capture precondition, restated as a mechanism because
  getting it wrong empties the region.  Counting the copier's own logging calls in
  this tree gives 12 at DEBUG, 8 at TRACE, 9 at INFO, 4 at WARN and 2 at ERROR,
  35 in total.  Of those, the ones that carry THIS region are the announcement at
  :779 and the completion at :796, both INFO, and the relayed child output, which
  goes out through the DEBUG-level adapter constructed at :790 and teed at :791 -
  the explanatory comment sits immediately above at :787-789 and the helper itself
  is declared at :777.  With the copier's logger pinned at warn, as described in
  skip-reason, INFO and DEBUG both fall below threshold, so the only lines this
  class could contribute at all are its two ERROR statements: the command-failure
  report at :802, which forced execution prevents from ever firing for a failed
  task, and the copy-abort report at :248, which fires only when the whole resource
  copy fails on an input-output error and then deliberately rethrows at :251 to
  stop the deployment.  A broken asset build under that configuration therefore
  leaves NO log trace whatsoever, which is a stronger statement than the evidence
  rule needs and the most direct possible argument for asserting on the artifacts
  instead.
- expected-evidence, and a place where this side's finding SUPERSEDES the
  counterpart's rather than reproducing it, so the difference is stated instead of
  being left to a diff.  At the base commit the copier had exactly TWO statements
  above DEBUG - one INFO recording the library folder path, at :114, and one ERROR
  on a copy failure, at :185 - and everything else, including all relayed pipeline
  output, went out at DEBUG.  In this tree that is no longer the shape: the census
  above records 15 statements above DEBUG, the library-folder INFO now sits at :188
  and the copy-failure ERROR at :248, and the announcements moved up to INFO while
  the relay itself stayed at DEBUG.  The PRECONDITION is unchanged in force even
  so, because the copier's logger is pinned above INFO as well as above DEBUG - and
  that is the point worth carrying forward: raising the announcements a level did
  not make this region capturable, so a reader must not infer from the base-commit
  finding that only DEBUG needs enabling.
- expected-evidence: three invocation pairs, in this order, each appearing as an
  About to run [{}] line followed by a done with [{}]: exit code {} line.  ONE, the
  dependency install, at AngularResourceCopier.java:216, from the install-command
  property configured at spring-web-ark-angular-starter.xml:68-70 with its value at
  :69 - the one command of the three that this migration REWROTE, now a clean,
  lockfile-respecting install.  TWO, the default asset task graph, at :231, from
  the property configured at :71-73 with its value at :72 - UNCHANGED by this
  migration.  THREE, the module-configuration merge, at :266, from the property
  configured at :45-47 with its value at :46, which invokes the task-runner target
  registered at Gruntfile.js:183 - also UNCHANGED.
- Governing rule on genuine target-runtime execution: the frontend must genuinely
  run on Node 20, with no container pinned to an old runtime and no vendored dead
  packages.  This capture is that rule's runtime proof, which is the entire reason
  the region is worth a file of its own.  The rule has a SECOND half that is just
  as binding and much easier to forget, and pairs TWO and THREE above are where it
  bites: it requires genuine target-runtime execution, NOT modernisation for its
  own sake.  The task runner and its command-line interface were assessed by
  installing AND executing them on the target runtime, they worked, and they were
  therefore deliberately held at the versions the base commit declares.  That is
  why pairs TWO and THREE are unchanged by decision rather than by omission, and
  why Gruntfile.js is byte-for-byte the file the counterpart region describes -
  every anchor in it re-verified at the same line number on both sides.
- Governing rule on justified change, and this is the note on holding versions
  referred to above: no dependency version changes without a specific
  compatibility reason for the target runtime.  Its CONTRAPOSITIVE is load-bearing
  and is recorded here deliberately, because it is the half most easily forgotten -
  HOLDING a version after assessment is as much a rule-compliant decision as
  changing one.  Bumping a component that had been proven to work would itself have
  breached the rule.  That is why this region's task graph, its task names and its
  five artifact names are expected to be identical on both sides of the mirror: the
  tool that produces them was not replaced.  The single command this migration did
  change carries its own compatibility reason, and that reason belongs to the
  dependency inventory rather than to this region.
- expected-evidence: the three property KEYS are the command identities the
  normalisation policy preserves, and all three are stable across the mirror -
  verified, the install property retains its original key in this tree even though
  its VALUE is replaced.  A reader comparing the two sides should therefore expect
  the same three keys in the same order, with a changed value on the first pair
  only.  The key is cited because it is the stable handle; it is not an endorsement
  of the tool its name recalls.
- expected-evidence: a non-obvious property of the THIRD pair that a mechanical
  diff will otherwise trip over.  The call at :266 sits inside a per-profile helper
  declared at :255 and invoked from the loop at :226-229, once for EACH active
  profile, and the active-profile list built at :220-222 is the extension profile
  when one is present plus a fixed custom profile.  The third pair therefore
  REPEATS, and its repetition count is an environment-dependent property of the
  capture rather than a defect.  A baseline and a migrated capture taken with
  different active profiles will differ in that count for a reason that carries no
  behavioural signal, so the profile set must match across the mirror.
- expected-evidence: what the FIRST pair is expected to show on this side, and the
  reason it is the whole point of the gate.  The install is expected to complete on
  the target Node.js runtime from the committed lockfile, resolving NO mutable
  reference: the lockfile is at version 3, and every source-control dependency
  specification in the frontend manifest carries an exact 40-character commit
  identifier.  Re-derived rather than adopted from the plan: the manifest holds 58
  such specifications, every one of them SHA-pinned, with ZERO specifications using
  a range-qualified fragment and ZERO carrying a fragment that is not a
  40-character commit identifier.  The pre-migration package manager is named in
  the counterpart region only as the thing observed at the base commit; it is NOT a
  prerequisite and NOT a recommendation, its removal is the point of this half of
  the migration, and it is replaced here by the clean lockfile-respecting install
  above.
- expected-evidence: the install is also expected to be preceded by a runtime
  check, which is what makes the Node 20 requirement real at runtime rather than
  merely declared.  The required majors are configured at
  spring-web-ark-angular-starter.xml:66 and :67 as 20 and 10, the copier asks each
  launcher for its own version and refuses to install on a different major, and it
  logs the resolved launcher and the satisfied version at :1041, :1178 and :1260.
  Those three lines are INFO and are therefore subject to the same threshold as
  everything else in this region.
- expected-evidence: the copy manifest and the Java predicate that shadows it both
  followed the package-manager change, and the four sibling property lists did not.
  The manifest at spring-web-ark-angular-starter.xml:111-121 now carries the
  incoming package manager's lockfile at :119 in place of the outgoing one, and the
  hardcoded keep-list predicate - which named the outgoing lockfile IN JAVA CODE -
  now names the incoming one, at AngularResourceCopier.java:315 inside the filter
  chain at :312-317, with the intent recorded in the comment at :309-311.  The four
  sibling lists are UNCHANGED: the resource folders at :74-87, the custom resource
  sources at :88-98, the assembled folders at :99-110 and the assembled files at
  :122-128.  Had the predicate not followed the manifest, the deployment would have
  deleted the lockfile out of the staging folder on every start and the install
  would have stopped being reproducible - a failure that no manifest diff would
  reveal.
- expected-evidence: the default task graph is frozen contract.  Gruntfile.js:376
  registers it as exactly loadConfig, clean, ngAnnotate, uglify, concat, cssmin,
  renderHome, cacheBust, copyToModulesConfigFolder - nine tasks, in that order.
  Per-task progress lines are captured where the log carries them, with task names,
  graph order and per-task file counts preserved and the clock normalised.  Nothing
  in this file proposes a change to that graph.
- expected-evidence: the five output artifact names the graph produces, with their
  re-derived anchors, because the names are contract under the asset-layout rule -
  assets/dist/application.js at Gruntfile.js:69, assets/dist/application.min.js at
  :55, assets/dist/vendors.min.js at :76, assets/dist/application.min.css at :62,
  and the rewritten home.html at :84.  They are named here so the region can be
  read.  Their digests belong to the sibling artifacts/ directory, referenced by
  directory NAME ONLY: this region computes no digest and quotes no digest value,
  because duplicating one would create two authorities for the same fact.  A reader
  who wants to know whether the asset build worked must read that directory, not
  this one.
- Governing rule on the asset-layout contract: the task graph, the asset globs and
  the five output artifact names are contract, and every dependency key is
  immutable because the asset resolver holds a long list of literal
  dependency-directory paths, most of them under one scoped prefix.  No key may be
  renamed and the install topology may not be flattened; the concatenation order
  that produces the five artifacts derives from those globs, so a renamed key or a
  flattened tree would change the bytes while the build still succeeded.  This
  region observes that contract and does not restate the list, which belongs to the
  frozen configuration tree rather than to an evidence file.
- expected-evidence, determinism finding 1 of 4: cache busting is CONTENT-HASH
  based.  Its configuration at Gruntfile.js:79-86 operates over the distribution
  directory, declared at :82, and rewrites the home page, declared at :84, with no
  timestamp, no build banner and no date injection anywhere in the file.  Repeated
  builds of identical input therefore produce identical output.
- expected-evidence, determinism finding 2 of 4: minification runs with identifier
  mangling DISABLED, at Gruntfile.js:51, which removes the principal source of
  benign inter-build variance.
- expected-evidence, determinism finding 3 of 4: concatenation order derives from
  the frozen asset globs, so preserving every dependency key and the install
  topology means that order cannot drift.  The clean task also wipes the
  distribution directory first, configured at Gruntfile.js:131, so a stale artefact
  cannot masquerade as a match.
- expected-evidence, determinism finding 4 of 4, and it is an honest caveat stated
  rather than glossed: source-map generation is ENABLED, at Gruntfile.js:52, and a
  source map embeds file paths.  The comparison build must therefore execute from
  the SAME relative path, or the map file will differ while the JavaScript bundles
  do not.  The byte comparison is scoped to the five artifacts accordingly, and the
  map is treated as path-sensitive rather than behaviour-bearing.  That caveat's
  full treatment, including the scope it settles, belongs to the sibling notes/
  directory and is referenced here by directory name rather than restated.
- expected-evidence: an environment input that conditions the pipeline.  The
  development value of the node-environment variable is exported in the production
  launch configuration described below, and it reaches exactly one branch:
  Gruntfile.js:163 tests the variable for the exact value production when the
  renderHome task decides which asset lists to render into the home page, so any
  other value, including an unset variable, follows the same development branch.
  It is recorded as an input to this capture because it selects the content of one
  of the five artifacts, and because the value in force must match across the
  mirror for the comparison to mean anything.
- expected-evidence, stated as a fact about a MECHANISM and not as an action
  performed here: the generated configuration module the build requires is written
  into the staging folder by AngularResourceCopier.java, in the helper declared at
  :269 with the exported shape built at :271-273, and the call site is at :224 -
  between the install at :216 and the task graph at :231, which is the only place
  it can sit and still be present when the graph runs.  This file neither creates
  that module nor asks for it to be created; it records how the writer behaves.
- expected-evidence: warning and error lines from the pipeline are captured AS
  OBSERVED, with their message text preserved in full and nothing repaired, nothing
  retried into submission and nothing hidden.  The consolidated error-severity scan
  is a separate sibling region and is not duplicated here.
- Governing rule on discovered defects, verbatim: "Pre-existing bugs discovered
  during the work are documented rather than fixed, unless one blocks a validation
  item."  Applied to this region that means the two aliases above are registered
  and left alone, and every warning or error the pipeline emits would be captured
  exactly as observed.  The escape clause in that rule is invoked EXACTLY TWICE in
  the entire change set.  Both invocations are frontend-related, which is why they
  are worth naming as a boundary in this, the frontend region: one concerns a
  generated configuration module that the build requires and version control did
  not carry, the other the removal of a build package that cannot be compiled on
  the target runtime.  NEITHER invocation is in this folder, and nothing in this
  file performs, proposes or implies either of them - nor any other repair, to the
  two task aliases or to anything else.
- Governing rule on ambiguity: the application's observed behaviour at the base
  commit on the older runtime is the tie-breaker for any ambiguity, and each
  resolution must be documented.  Paraphrased deliberately rather than quoted,
  because the rule's own text names that runtime with a phrase this documentation
  tree's wording gate forbids; where the tree needs to name it, it reads JDK 8.
  The substitution changes no meaning and is disclosed here rather than made
  silently.
- That rule carries unusual weight for this side of the migration.  Re-verified in
  this tree: the frontend contains ZERO specification files, despite declaring a
  test runner and an assertion library among its build dependencies.  A search of
  the frontend resource tree for specification files returns nothing at all.  There
  is therefore no automated behavioural test for the frontend to fall back on, and
  artifact-level identity against the captured baseline is the strongest
  behavioural evidence this side of the migration has.  That is exactly why a
  hollow capture would be so damaging here, and why an absence is recorded in this
  region instead of a plausible-looking substitute: an unrun pipeline's behaviour
  is unknowable rather than assumable.
- Governing rule on the frontend dependency graph: the graph must be fully
  reproducible from a committed lockfile, with no dependency resolving differently
  between two checkouts.  Its bearing on this region is indirect but real - it is
  what makes the concatenation order that produces these artifacts stable across
  runs, and therefore what makes a byte comparison meaningful at all.  The lockfile
  itself is out of this region's territory: no lockfile content, no dependency
  specification and no dependency tree appears here beyond the single pinning fact
  recorded above.  The inventory is a separate deliverable.
- Governing rule that nothing is silently skipped: honoured by making this skip
  loud.  region-status is a fixed vocabulary of exactly CAPTURED or SKIPPED,
  skip-reason names the specific unmet preconditions and distinguishes them from
  one another, and the delimiters are present and empty rather than absent.  A
  reader or a script can tell in one line that this region carries no observation,
  which is the opposite of a silent skip.  An empty region is not presented here as
  a clean one.
- Governing rule against relying on JDK-internal access in production launch
  configuration.  Verified after migration as well as at the base commit: that
  configuration contains ZERO module-access JVM arguments.  Had such an argument
  appeared in a captured banner it would be replaced, in this region, by the
  description "a module-access JVM argument that opens or exports an
  otherwise-encapsulated JDK package to the unnamed module", and the substitution
  would be recorded in this notes block and in the redacted field.  None arose, so
  none was made.  The register of applied exceptions is referred to BY TITLE ONLY,
  as the JDK access exceptions register, because its filename embeds a token this
  tree excludes; it is delivered empty with a positive statement that none are
  required.
- Registered observation, not a defect report against this file and not something
  to fix: the two recursive scans for module-access tokens that this deliverable is
  checked against cannot return zero across the whole documentation tree, because
  the register just referred to carries such a token inside its own required
  filename and three pages cite it by that name - the top-level readme, the setup
  page and the dependency inventory.  Re-measured in this tree: three matching
  lines across the documentation tree and the readme, one of them under the
  migration folder, and ZERO anywhere under this evidence folder.  Those files are
  outside this file's scope and were not modified.  Within
  docs/migration/smoke-evidence the count is zero, and this file keeps it zero.
- Governing rule on namespace invariance: it is honoured here trivially and
  completely, in that this region names no package namespace at all and contains no
  reference to the namespace the migration excludes.  The excluded frontend
  technologies are likewise absent - a different frontend framework, a typed
  dialect of the language, a module-bundling toolchain and any interface redesign -
  and nothing here reads as advocacy for any of them.  The pipeline this region
  observes is the existing task-runner pipeline, unchanged.
- Credential posture, in the repository's own voice rather than a policy invented
  here.  docs/setup.md states, at the security note following its own launch
  configuration, that the default administrator credential is for local developer
  evaluation only, that it must be rotated in any shared, staging or production
  deployment, and that credentials should be injected through environment
  variables, encrypted configuration values or a secret manager and never
  committed to source control; that page already renders the administrator password
  as <REDACTED>, where the top-level readme does not.  This capture honours that
  posture and reuses that token.  Measured rather than asserted: the documented
  administrator password occurs in exactly ONE file in the whole tracked tree, the
  top-level readme, and in ZERO files anywhere under the migration folder - and
  this file keeps that count at zero by never transcribing it.
- The launch configuration audited for the rule on JDK-internal access is the
  fenced shell block in the top-level readme, duplicated in docs/setup.md.  It is
  described here and cited by location, and deliberately never reproduced verbatim,
  for two reasons: it carries credential-shaped values, and it also carries
  pre-existing unbalanced quotation marks - confirmed by counting quotation marks
  per line, three on the line that exports the JVM options and one unmatched on
  each of the two lines that export the container options.  Importing that block
  would copy a pre-existing defect into a new file for no benefit, and the rule on
  discovered defects forbids repairing it, so it is neither repaired nor imported.
  The node-environment value recorded above is exported from inside that same
  block, which is why the block is cited at all.
- Independent corroboration from elsewhere in this evidence tree, cited with
  attribution so it is not mistaken for this region.  The sibling
  notes/frontend-comparison-provenance.txt records that the five artifact digests
  for this side came from a STANDALONE command-line pipeline run in this checkout on
  the target runtime, installing cleanly from the committed lockfile, and it records
  in its own words that forced execution means a task failure can still exit zero,
  which is why the gate it feeds asserts on digests rather than on an exit status.
  That run had no container and no copier, so it is a DIFFERENT producer and a
  different territory: it is corroboration for the mechanism and for the toolchain,
  not a capture of this region, and a reader must not extend it into one.  The
  outcome of that comparison, including the residual differences it reports and
  where they are registered, belongs to that note and to the migration's own
  registers rather than to this region.
- Mirror obligation.  The counterpart is baseline/startup/frontend-build.log: the
  same filename, the same field set in the same order including
  exit-status-is-not-evidence, and the same region-selection rule stated above, so
  that a recursive diff between the two capture directories is mechanically
  meaningful.  Only two values differ by design, runtime and commit, plus the one
  re-measured precondition difference set out in skip-reason and the anchor drift
  disclosed above.  A field added on one side only, or a region selected differently
  on one side only, would register as a difference that carries no behavioural
  signal at all.
